    basic: "Avoid the 'Danger' rows. If a row already has 5 cards, do not play a card that is just slightly higher than the 5th cardâ€”you will almost certainly be forced to take it. Instead, play a very high card that will land in a 'Short' row, or a very low card to 'Flush' a low-point row if you know you are going to lose anyway.",
    intermediate: "The 'Hole' Play: If you have a sequence (e.g., 22, 23, 24), you can safely play them over multiple turns if the last number in a row is something like 20. Because you know nobody else can play a card BETWEEN 20 and 22, you won't be pushed into the 6th spot by an opponent. Create your own safe gaps.",
    advanced: "Crowd-Psychology Blocking: In a 10-player game, rows fill up instantly. If you see many players are low on cards, they will likely play high. Use this to your advantage by playing consistently low, forcing THEM to take the 10-point rows while you take a single 1-point card to reset a row early. Knowing when to 'take a small hit' to avoid a 'massive blowout' is the key to winning Peeso.",
    tips: [
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Cards ending in 5 or 0, and double numbers (like 55), usually have more 'Horns' (points). Avoid taking these at all costs.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Calculated Suicide: If you have to take a row, pick the one with the fewest points, even if it's not the one your card technically belonged to (if you were too low to play anywhere else).",
      },
